  "_payers_comment": "age_shares override the marginal share and are renormalised per band; Medicare eligibility at 65 is the single sharpest discontinuity in US healthcare data and any tool that has never seen it will mis-handle real Medicare cohorts. Coverage is emitted as payer_plan_period rows, with churn: commercial plans lapse and switch at open enrolment, which creates the coverage gaps that make continuous-enrolment criteria bite.",

  "coverage": {
    "_comment": "How enrolment spells behave. Real coverage is not one unbroken span per patient: people change jobs, plans reset in January, Medicaid redetermination drops people mid-year. Spell boundaries clustering on 1 January is a real and recognisable artifact.",
    "mean_spell_years": {"commercial": 3.1, "medicare": 9.0, "medicaid": 2.2, "self_pay": 1.4},
    "p_january_boundary": 0.63,
    "p_gap_after_spell": {"commercial": 0.18, "medicare": 0.02, "medicaid": 0.31, "self_pay": 0.45},
    "gap_days_mean": 95,
    "_active_comment": "Share of each payer's members still enrolled on the extract date. An extract's population is drawn from whoever the payer covers now, so most of it must reach the extract date; spells built forward from an early start and abandoned at the first lapse left the file with almost no data in its final years, which is the one period an analyst is most likely to query.",
    "p_active_at_extract": {"commercial": 0.62, "medicare": 0.88, "medicaid": 0.55, "self_pay": 0.34}
  },

